<DocsLayout title="Self-hosting" activeNav="/self-hosting">
  <h1>Self-hosting</h1>
  <p>
    App Manager ships as a container image on GitHub Container Registry, plus a Compose stack that
    runs the web console, the queue worker, the scheduler, PostgreSQL and Redis. Everything the
    instance does — how credentials may be stored, whether secrets traverse the API, how the first
    owner comes into existence — is set through environment variables.
  </p>

  <h2>Quick start</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`mkdir app-manager && cd app-manager
curl -O https://raw.githubusercontent.com/karerckor/app-manager/main/deploy/docker/compose.yaml
curl -o .env https://raw.githubusercontent.com/karerckor/app-manager/main/deploy/docker/.env.example

echo "DB_PASSWORD=$(openssl rand -hex 32)" >> .env
echo "APP_KEY=$(docker run --rm --entrypoint app-manager ghcr.io/karerckor/app-manager-platform:latest generate-key)" >> .env

docker compose up -d`} />
  <p>
    Open <code>http://localhost:8000</code>. The installer creates your first owner and team.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong><code>APP_KEY</code> is not a formality.</strong> Every credential in the vault is
    encrypted with it. Store it wherever you keep production secrets and back it up with the
    database — a dump without the key cannot be decrypted.
  </p>
  <p>
    See <a href="/security">Security</a> before rotating <code>APP_KEY</code>. Credential data uses
    envelope encryption, while integration secrets and sessions may still need an older key through
    <code>APP_PREVIOUS_KEYS</code> during the transition.
  </p>
  <p>
    Before starting a real instance, choose its bootstrap, credential storage, and credential API
    profile in <a href="/deployment-modes">Deployment modes and Installer</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What runs</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Service</th><th>Role</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>init</code></td><td>One-shot: runs migrations, then <code>encryption:migrate</code> to ensure the instance has an active credential encryption key, then the first-run bootstrap, then exits. Everything else waits for it.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>app</code></td><td>nginx + PHP-FPM serving the console and the REST API.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>queue</code></td><td>Worker for the <code>notifications</code>, <code>integrations</code>, <code>webhooks</code> and <code>default</code> queues.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>scheduler</code></td><td>Build reconciliation, retention and cold-archive transitions, permanent trash purges, credential expiry checks.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>postgres</code>, <code>redis</code></td><td>Database and cache. Point the stack at managed instances by changing <code>DB_HOST</code> / <code>REDIS_HOST</code> and removing these services.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>caddy</code></td><td>Optional. <code>docker compose --profile tls up -d</code> terminates TLS for <code>APP_DOMAIN</code>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    <code>queue</code> and <code>scheduler</code> use process healthchecks. Confirm both are healthy
    with <code>docker compose ps</code>; use <code>docker compose logs queue scheduler</code> when
    either process is restarting or unhealthy.
  </p>

  <h2>Startup presets</h2>
  <p>
    These settings are independent, so a bootstrap mode can be combined with any credential
    storage/API profile. See <a href="/deployment-modes">Deployment modes and Installer</a> for the
    exact behavior, examples, and the difference between demo bootstrap and database seeding.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Goal</th><th>Variables</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Configure in the browser (default)</td><td><code>APP_MANAGER_BOOTSTRAP=installer</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>First owner straight from the environment</td><td><code>APP_MANAGER_BOOTSTRAP=admin</code> with <code>ADMIN_EMAIL</code>, <code>ADMIN_PASSWORD</code>, <code>ADMIN_NAME</code>, <code>ADMIN_TEAM_NAME</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Throwaway demo instance</td><td><code>APP_MANAGER_BOOTSTRAP=demo</code> together with <code>APP_ENV=local</code> — seeds <code>demo@example.com</code> / <code>Demo123!</code> and the <code>demo-api-key</code> API key</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Bring your own vault only</td><td><code>CREDENTIAL_STORAGE_MODES=external</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Platform-held secrets only</td><td><code>CREDENTIAL_STORAGE_MODES=local</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Both (default)</td><td><code>CREDENTIAL_STORAGE_MODES=local,external</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Secrets never traverse the API</td><td><code>CREDENTIALS_API_PROFILE=dashboard_only</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Only <code>demo</code> generates its own <code>APP_KEY</code> when you haven't supplied one. It
    stores that key in the <code>storage-app</code> volume, so restarts and upgrades reuse it, and
    the data is disposable anyway. The key reaches the application through the config cache the
    entrypoint builds at startup — so if you run <code>php artisan config:clear</code> inside a
    running container, restart it afterwards instead of leaving it up. Every other mode refuses to
    start without <code>APP_KEY</code>, on purpose. Demo mode also requires
    <code>APP_ENV=local</code> — the demo seeder is disabled in production environments, and
    <code>app:bootstrap</code> fails loudly rather than leaving you with an empty instance that looks
    healthy.
  </p>

  <h2>TLS</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`docker compose --profile tls up -d`} />
  <p>
    Caddy obtains and renews a certificate for <code>APP_DOMAIN</code> and proxies to
    <code>app:8080</code>. Uncomment the whole TLS block in <code>.env</code>, not only
    <code>APP_DOMAIN</code> — the later definitions override the defaults higher up in the file:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="dotenv" code={`APP_DOMAIN=appmanager.example.com
APP_URL=https://appmanager.example.com
APP_PORT=127.0.0.1:8000
SESSION_SECURE_COOKIE=true
TRUSTED_PROXIES=*`} />
  <p>
    <code>APP_URL</code> has to move with the scheme and host. Left at
    <code>http://localhost:8000</code> it breaks signed URLs, password-reset and invitation links,
    and the GitHub App redirect URI.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong><code>APP_PORT=127.0.0.1:8000</code> is part of the security posture, not a
    convenience.</strong> <code>TRUSTED_PROXIES=*</code> tells App Manager to believe the
    <code>X-Forwarded-For</code> and <code>X-Forwarded-Proto</code> headers of whoever connected, so
    it is only acceptable while Caddy is the only thing that can reach the app. Binding the
    plaintext port to loopback is what makes that true. Publish it on
    <code>0.0.0.0</code> alongside <code>TRUSTED_PROXIES=*</code> and a client can pick the IP
    address App Manager throttles its login attempts by and records as the actor in the
    credential-access audit log.
  </p>

  <h2>Environment reference</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Variable</th><th>Default</th><th>What it does</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>APP_KEY</code></td><td>—</td><td>Required. Encrypts credentials, sessions and stored integration secrets.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>APP_PREVIOUS_KEYS</code></td><td>empty</td><td>Comma-separated older <code>APP_KEY</code> values used to decrypt data that has not yet been rewritten after a key rotation.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>APP_URL</code></td><td><code>http://localhost:8000</code></td><td>Public URL. Must match the scheme users actually use.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>APP_PORT</code></td><td><code>8000</code></td><td>Host port for the console.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>DB_PASSWORD</code></td><td>—</td><td>Required. Password for the bundled PostgreSQL user; Compose refuses to start without it.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>APP_MANAGER_BOOTSTRAP</code></td><td><code>installer</code></td><td><code>installer</code>, <code>admin</code> or <code>demo</code>. Anything but <code>installer</code> disables the web installer.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>CREDENTIAL_STORAGE_MODES</code></td><td><code>local,external</code></td><td>Instance-wide ceiling on credential storage modes. A team may narrow it, never widen it.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>CREDENTIALS_API_PROFILE</code></td><td><code>full</code></td><td><code>dashboard_only</code> removes every endpoint that moves secret material.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>ALLOW_PUBLIC_REGISTRATION</code></td><td><code>false</code></td><td>Opens the public sign-up form. Leave closed on anything internet-facing.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>TRUSTED_PROXIES</code></td><td>empty</td><td><code>*</code> or a comma-separated list. Set only when a reverse proxy terminates TLS.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>MEDIA_STORAGE_DRIVER</code></td><td><code>local</code></td><td><code>local</code> keeps files in the <code>storage-app</code> volume; <code>s3</code> uses the <code>MEDIA_S3_*</code> variables.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>TRASH_RETENTION_DAYS</code></td><td><code>90</code></td><td>Minimum recovery window before soft-deleted applications, identifiers, profiles, builds, artifacts, blueprints, teams and users are permanently purged. Parents remain longer when a child cold archive is still retained.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>CREDENTIAL_TRASH_RETENTION_DAYS</code></td><td><code>90</code></td><td>Independent recovery window for credentials and their private files.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>BUILD_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS</code></td><td><code>30</code></td><td>Hot database window for completed build logs.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>BUILD_LOG_ARCHIVE_RETENTION_DAYS</code></td><td><code>365</code></td><td>Cold private-storage window for build logs.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>SENTRY_HOT_RETENTION_DAYS</code></td><td><code>30</code></td><td>Hot database window for terminal Sentry envelope and event payloads.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>SENTRY_ARCHIVE_RETENTION_DAYS</code></td><td><code>365</code></td><td>Cold private-storage window before archived telemetry and its database metadata are purged.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>SENTRY_ARCHIVE_DISK</code></td><td>media disk</td><td>Optional Laravel filesystem disk dedicated to compressed Sentry archives.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>DB_HOST</code>, <code>REDIS_HOST</code></td><td><code>postgres</code>, <code>redis</code></td><td>Point at managed services to drop the bundled containers.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Data retention and recovery</h2>
  <p>
    Deleting an application, credential, identifier, profile, build, artifact, blueprint, team or
    user moves it to recoverable trash. The daily scheduler permanently removes the database rows
    and private files only after the configured trash window. Applications and credentials can be
    restored through the console or API during that window.
  </p>
  <p>
    Completed build logs and terminal Sentry payloads first remain hot in PostgreSQL, then move to
    private storage; Sentry archives are compressed and checksummed. Sentry keeps searchable issue
    and event metadata after the raw envelope is cold; authorized users can still download the complete archive. At the end of the
    cold window, the scheduler deletes both the object and the corresponding retained records.
    Monitor scheduler health and storage capacity, and include every configured archive disk in
    backups for the full promised retention period.
  </p>

  <h2>Upgrading</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`docker compose pull && docker compose up -d`} />
  <p>
    <code>init</code> re-runs migrations and <code>encryption:migrate</code> before the new
    containers start serving; both are idempotent. Pin a version
    instead of <code>latest</code> — <code>ghcr.io/karerckor/app-manager-platform:1.4</code> — if you
    want upgrades to be a deliberate edit.
  </p>

  <h2>Backups</h2>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`docker compose exec -T postgres pg_dump -U app_manager app_manager | gzip > app-manager-$(date +%F).sql.gz
docker run --rm -v app-manager_storage-app:/data -v "$PWD:/backup" alpine \\
  tar czf /backup/app-manager-storage-$(date +%F).tar.gz -C /data .`} />
  <p>
    The database holds everything except uploaded files; the <code>storage-app</code> volume holds
    keystores, certificates and build artifacts when <code>MEDIA_STORAGE_DRIVER=local</code>. Keep
    <code>APP_KEY</code> alongside both.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you replace that named volume with a host directory, <code>chown -R 33:33</code> it first. The
    container runs unprivileged as <code>www-data</code> (uid 33) and cannot take ownership of a
    root-owned bind mount; named volumes inherit the right ownership from the image automatically.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
